cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "SPID: A Security Principle Instantiation Detector for Security Explanation Text"
message: "If you use this software, please cite both the software and the paper."
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: Brown
    given-names: Caitlin
    email: caitlin.n.brown@durham.ac.uk
    affiliation: Durham University
  - family-names: Morisset
    given-names: Charles
    email: charles.morisset@durham.ac.uk
    affiliation: Durham University
repository-code: "https://github.com/xsec-tools/spid"
abstract: >-
  SPID is a Security Principle Instantiation Detector for security explanation
  text. It labels each sentence-level unit of an explanation with the Basin et
  al. design principle it instantiates, abstaining when the text only describes
  a mechanism. A unit instantiates a principle when it argues that the design
  property the principle names improves security, not merely when it mentions a
  related concept. The tool combines a fine-tuned Claims-Evidence classifier
  with a three-stage principle detector built on Meta-Llama-3-70B-Instruct.
keywords:
  - explainable security
  - security design principles
  - argument mining
  - natural language processing
  - security explanation
license: MIT
